WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.037
The daily CTE produces one revenue value per date.

00:00:04.037 --> 00:00:11.222
The window AVG orders those dates and uses a frame from two preceding rows through the current row.

00:00:11.222 --> 00:00:16.682
Run the query and compare the raw daily revenue with the smoothed moving average.

00:00:16.682 --> 00:00:23.167
Remember that this is a three-row window, not necessarily three calendar days if dates are missing.

00:00:23.167 --> 00:00:28.172
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:28.172 --> 00:00:34.487
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:34.487 --> 00:00:37.117
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:37.117 --> 00:00:42.177
In a real setting such as a hospital ranking visits and identifying readmissions,

00:00:42.177 --> 00:00:48.417
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:48.417 --> 00:00:50.737
before discussing syntax.

00:00:50.737 --> 00:00:57.280
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
